otions  for example the story corresponding to  the sad expression was the person's  child had died and he felt sad  then they presented photos of three  different expressions with the story  that matched one of the expressions and  asked participants to match the story  with one of three expressions  this was a test of the decoding  hypothesis  in another task ekman and friesen  videotaped the foray participants as  they displayed facial expressions  displayed in response to the  emotion-specific story  they then presented these videotapes to  undergraduate students in the united  states and asked them to select from six  emotion charts the one that best matched  the fourier's pose  this was a test of the encoding  hypothesis  ekman and friesen found that the foray  participants accurately judged the  proper emotions corresponding to the  facial expressions  this served as evidence of the encoding  hypothesis  they also found that college students  from the united states correctly  interpreted the posed expressions of the  videotaped foray participants  this is evidence of the decoding  hypothesis  taken together these findings constitute  strong evidence for the universality of  facial expressions of emotion  now i would be remiss if i did not say  that these findings were not without  criticism  in fact the word remiss is certainly an  understatement because these findings  and the critique of these findings have  created a long-standing debate among  emotion researchers  here are a few of the main critiques  the first was in regard to the response  format that ekman and friesen chose to  use  they used a forced choice technique that  asked participants to label facial  expressions using a set of terms that  the researchers provided  it is possible that this inflated the  accuracy of participants judgments  because there were only a select few  possible choices  an open-ended response style would have  solved this problem though it would have  been much more diff