he  basic emotions  they include the inner eyebrow razor  most people can raise their inner  eyebrow concurrently with their outer  eyebrow however very few people can  raise their inner eyebrow independently  from their outer eyebrow unless they are  genuinely feeling fearful  they also include the lip corner  depressor which pulls the lip corners  downward in sadness  interestingly many fake this action by  raising their chin boss which is  detectably different  there is a video clip of staff sergeant  ryan pitts receiving a medal from  president barack obama in 2014.  at this moment obama says something that  would make the adolescent boy in all of  us chuckle  he says something along the lines of up  at their tiny post ryan and his team  were being pounded  if you look closely you can see that  sergeant pitts smiles slightly before  voluntarily counteracting the upward  polar lip corner with other facial  muscles  this has always been fascinating to me  because it highlights our inability to  control our facial expressions  completely  more specifically if sergeant pitts was  able to stop a smile he would have  the fact that he has to recruit  additional muscles to counteract the  smile suggested he cannot simply stop  the muscles associated with the smile  from contracting  this is what antonio dimaggio called  controlling the uncontrollable he said  and i quote  we are about as effective at stopping an  emotion as we are preventing a sneeze we  can try to prevent the expression of  emotion and we may succeed in part but  not in full end quote  so taken together we have seen that the  face reveals a great deal of information  and that facial expressions are  difficult to fake  but why  why are emotions so intimately tied to  our faces and the way that we move them  asked in a different way  what's the benefit of honestly  advertising something as personal  subjective and intimate as your own  emotional and motivational states  someplace as conspicuous as the face  again a place so conspicuous that it's  hard to inconspicuously hide it  wouldn't it be better to just keep your  emotional states private wouldn't a  poker face be better  wouldn't the proverbial smell of fear  just egg on our opponents  now  the benefit to the person who sees the  facial expression is clear  that person gets accurate information  regarding the signalers emotional state  which is tied to their action tendencies  but based off of evolutionary theory and  signaling theory there needs to be a  benefit to the signaler or the facial  expresser him or herself  this is a question that i put forth a  lot of effort in studying and i think  we've got some good answers  one of the answers might be that facial  expressions serve to advertise  subjective commitments  there are class of problems that the  economist thomas shelling called  commitment problems  let me give you a few examples  a kidnapper suddenly gets cold feet  he wants to set his victim free but he's  afraid that his victim will go to the  police if he does  in return for his freedom the victim  gladly promises that he'll never go to  the police  the problem is this  both the kidnapper and the victim  realize that it will no longer be in the  victim's interest to keep this promise  once the victim is set free  so the kidnapper reluctantly concludes  that he has to kill the victim  how might you solve this problem  well here's one solution  if the victim has committed an act whose  disclosure could lead to blackmail he  might confess it  if not he might commit one in the  presence of the kidnapper to create a  bond that will ensure the victim's  silence  perhaps the victim could allow the  kidnapper to photograph him or her in  the process of some unspeakably  degrading or embarrassing act  the threat of blackmail hero serves as a  commitment device  a commitment device is something that  provides the victim with an incentive to  keep his or her promise after they've  been set free  surely opening up the threat of black  men will certainly be unpleasant for the  victim once he or she is freed but  clearly less so than not being able to  make a credible promise in the first  place and being killed  these types of commitment devices are  everywhere and they work just as well  when they commit ourselves to specific  actions as they are when they commit  others to specific actions  for example the best way to make sure  that you actually get up on time is to  voluntarily place your alarm clock on  the other side of the room  the best way to make sure that you won't  be late is to set your clock five  minutes fast  there are even computer and phone apps  that commit us to not wasting our time  using our cellular phones  here's another interpersonal example  let's say that you have two persons  named smith and jones  smith and jones can engage in a  potentially profitable venture let's say  opening up a restaurant  their potential gain arises from the  natural advantages inherent in the  division and specialization of labor  smith is a talented cook but is shy and  an incompetent manager  jones on the other hand cannot boil an  egg but is charming and has shrewd  business judgment  together they have the necessary skills  to launch a successful venture  working alone however their potential is  much more limited  the problem in this situation is that  each person will have opportunities to  cheat without the possibility of being  detected  smith can take kickbacks from food  suppliers and jones can skim the cash  drawer without smith's knowledge  if one of them cheats the cheater does  very well however the non-cheater does  poorly and cannot be sure why  the low return from the restaurant is  not a reliable sign as having been  cheated since there are many benign  explanations for why a business might do  poorly  if the victim also cheats he too can  escape detection and will do better by  cheating  this problem could be solved in the same  way as the kidnapper problem  if both smith and jones could make a  binding commitment not to cheat then  their partner would be happy to enter  into the cooperative venture with them  and they would both profit by doing so  as an economist and philosopher one of  adam smith's most enduring contributions  is his idea of moral sentiments  moral sentiments are emotions such as  anger contempt disgust envy greed shame  and guilt  smith argued that these sentiments  function by competing with calculations  stemming from rational self-interest  and it is for exactly this reason that  they can help people solve commitment  problems  consider a person capable of strong  feelings of guilt  this person will not cheat on their  partner even when it is in their  material interest to do so not because  he or she fears being caught but simply  because he or she doesn't want to cheat  the aversion to feelings of guilt  effectively alters the payoffs that he  or she faces  that is they know that cheating will  bring on awful feelings of guilt which  are so averse that they will deter the  individual from cheating in the first  place  now consider the person who becomes  enraged when dealt with unjustly  this person doesn't need a formal  contract to seek revenge they're going  to seek revenge because they want to  even when in purely material terms it  does not pay  here the feelings of anger offset their  material interests  the good feelings of exacting vengeance  altered the payoffs that he or she faces  that is they know that exacting revenge  on the people who have dealt with them  unjustly motivate them to seek revenge  even when they have nothing to gain  now  the good feelings that we get by  remaining faithful to our partners and  by exacting revenge on the people who  have caused harm to us are in a very  real sense their own reward  but we are living in a material world a  world of material payoffs and for these  sentiments to be viable that is for them  to have evolved via evolution by natural  selection they must have yielded some  material payoff  let's take guilt for example  the potential gain of not cheating on  your partner is to motivate them to  engage in the cooperative venture with  you  not cheating is counterproductive after  the fact  let's take the example of revenge  a desire to seek revenge is  counterproductive after the fact  what good is it proving to someone now  that they should not have harmed you  then  so it's not enough for us to have these  subjective commitments they have to be  honestly communicated for them to incur  a benefit  and that is why we express them on our  faces  this idea is no better expressed than in  this 1971 comic from the new yorker  magazine  here you have a couple walking down the  street who needs to decide whether or  not to buy a pencil from the  extortionist who is carrying a  collections box and a whip  if they believe the extortion is to be a  rational person then the whip doesn't  matter there's no way that a rational  person would risk a jail sentence for a  few added pencil sales  however if they believe the extortion is  to be an irrational person then it is in  their self-interest to buy a pencil  whether they want one or not  notice here that it isn't the sign  around his neck that lets them know that  he's not rational that sign can be  easily fixed  what lets them know for sure is a look  on his face  a look that expresses an emotion that he  did not decide to have and cannot decide  not to have  this idea provides an explanation for  why emotions are so intimately tied to  the body  it has been proposed independently by  the economist thomas schelling the  evolutionary psychologist robert trivers  martin daley and margot wilson and the  economists robert frank and jack  hirschlifer  it goes by several names the most common  are the dooms in machine theory the  theory of mutually assured destruction  and the frank hirschlifer hypothesis  my colleagues and i have tested this  hypothesis over the past several years  in these studies we have found that  smiles honestly signal a commitment to  behave cooperatively towards others  angry expressions honestly signal a  commitment to behave spitefully towards  others  sadness expressions honestly signaled  the need for help and fearful  expressions honestly signal the presence  of danger  each of these studies has demonstrated  that people respond to these expressions  differently in comparison to a neutral  expression  taken together with a theory that i just  described they strongly suggest that  facial expressions honestly advertise  subjective commitments  this idea makes sense intuitively  one day i went into the fabric store to  get a frame for a radiohead poster i'd  purchased at a concert  i went up to the register and was  prepared to lay down the 100 or so  dollars for the frame  however the cashier to my surprise told  me that there was a sale and the frame  was 50 off  the store giving me the frame for 50 off  at this point was clearly not in their  self-interest  i was going to buy the frame anyways and  was prepared to give them the full price  the whole point of a sale is to get  people to buy things that they might not  normally buy at full price  it's a commitment  but like the subjective commitments that  we've talked about before it's useless  after the fact and needs to be honestly  advertised to benefit the person willing  to make the commitment  this is why sales are so ubiquitously  advertised both before and during the  time they are being held  so we've talked about non-verbal  behavior in general and facial  expressions of emotion in particular  i also presented a theory that explains  why emotions are expressed on the face  and in the body  if you're interested in learning more  about this i would highly recommend the  1988 book by robert frank called  passions within reason or the edited  volume by randy nessie called evolution  and the capacity for commitment  here we discuss the expressions of  emotions and the ways that others can be  conscious of them  in the next lecture we'll talk about the  self-conscious emotions that we are  inherently aware of in ourselves  [Music]  you
 

